<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1483">1483</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Zahir-ud-din Muhammad Babur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babur was a Timurid prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. After losing his homeland in Fergana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, he established himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. In 1526 he defeated the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> at the Battle of Panipat, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, and his memoir, the Baburnama, is a famous autobiographical work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babur.jpg" alt="Babur" width="270" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: c.1650 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humayun_and_Babur_(Late_Shah_Jahan_Album)_Babur_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last, Kalimullah, fled Bidar around 1527 and sought help from <b>Babur</b>, the new Mughal ruler of Delhi, and later from Bijapur and Ahmadnagar, dying in exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The rise of the Mughals and Afghans in the north brought harder enemies: <b>Babur</b> defeated a Bengal army at the Battle of the Ghaghra in 1529, and Sher Khan Sur defeated the sultan's forces at Surajgarh in 1534 and twice besieged Gaur, taking it in 1538 despite Portuguese…</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…as a legitimising figurehead, continued to underpin political legitimacy in Central Asia long after the khanate's effective dissolution: <b>Babur</b>, founder of the Mughal Empire in India (1526), was a Timurid on his father's side and a descendant of the Chagataid khans through his mother, making the Chagatai inheritance…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Panipat on 21 April 1526, where Ibrahim Lodi's much larger army, reportedly some 100,000 strong, was destroyed by <b>Babur</b>'s combination of field guns and matchlocks behind a barrier of linked carts and the flanking tulughma manoeuvre — among the first decisive uses of gunpowder artillery in north Indian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Babur</b> descended from Timur on his father's side and from Genghis Khan on his mother's, and his dynasty's name comes from the Persian word for Mongol.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad Shaybani Khan's campaigns of 1500–1507 drove the Timurid prince <b>Babur</b> from Samarkand and Transoxiana and extinguished the Herat branch, pushing Babur south to Kabul and ultimately to found the Mughal Empire in India, the final and most enduring expression of the Timurid military tradition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
